course: dictionary
lesson: 12391
title: Cum: one thing and another
level: primary-6
stars: 0
explain: «Cum» is a small word, and it is a preposition, so it links one thing to another. In English you mostly see it joined with a dash, as in «X-cum-Y», and it means one thing that is also another thing. «The room is a kitchen-cum-dining room» means it is a kitchen and a dining room at the same time.
explain: Use the words on each side of «cum» to work out its meaning. «The old barn is a shed-cum-workshop» has a shed on one side and a workshop on the other, so the barn does two jobs: storing things and mending things. Check it by swapping in «and». If «a shed and a workshop» still makes sense, you have the right meaning.
explain: In Latin, «cum» means «with», and you will meet it in school and university honours. «Cum laude» means «with praise», and «magna cum laude» means «with great praise». Students get these honours for very good work.
fact: «Cum» is a preposition, a word that shows how one thing relates to another.
fact: In English, «X-cum-Y» means one thing that is also another, such as «a kitchen-cum-dining room».
fact: «Cum» comes from Latin, where it means «with».
fact: «Cum laude» means «with praise»; «magna cum laude» means «with great praise»; «summa cum laude» means «with the highest praise».
fact: Some English place names use it too, such as «Chorlton-cum-Hardy» in Manchester, where two villages are joined into one name.
exercise: «The garage is a workshop-cum-storeroom.» What does this mean? | *One room that is both a workshop and a storeroom | A room with no door | A garage that is closed for the day | Two garages joined by a road
exercise: In Latin, «cum» means which word? | Under | *With | After | Before
exercise: «Magna cum laude» means which of these? | Great music | Without any praise | With a big laugh | *With great praise
test: «The loft is a bedroom-cum-office.» What does this tell you? | A bedroom with a locked door | A small bedroom in the hall | *A room that is both a bedroom and an office | An office with no windows
test: Which phrase means «with great praise»? | Laude alone | Summa cum laude is not this one | Cum laude | *Magna cum laude
exam: «The cafe is a bookshop-cum-cafe.» Which phrase best explains it? | A library for young children | *A bookshop that is also a cafe | A bus stop with books on it | A shop with no books and no cafe
